  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 316 AM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nKMQT radar returns at 05Z show a bit more widespread rain shower \ncoverage over the western UP than forecast 24 hours ago as the \nshortwave low over northern Manitoba has more pronounced troughing \nalong its southern periphery. Despite this trend upwards in shower \ncoverage, intensity remains low as the showers are having to fight \nsubsidence from a surface high of 1026-1028 mb that remains over the \nregion, though is now centered around southern James Bay. 00Z HREF \n75th percentile QPF by the time showers end around 12Z is still less \nthan a tenth of an inch for all except for the Watersmeet area. \nConfidence in any measurable rainfall this morning decreases sharply \nover the east half of the UP, with the HREF PoPs east of the M-94 \ncorridor mostly sub-15%. The additional low level moisture from \nthese showers may lead to a few patches of fog, but widespread dense \nfog is not expected as HREF chances of visibility less than 1 mile \nis only 10-20% this morning.\n\nElsewhere, the 500mb pattern consists of 600 dam ridging over \nMemphis bounded by deep troughs centered over northern California \nand the Bahamas in addition to the aforementioned northern Manitoba \ntrough. The first key feature to watch will be a ridge-riding \nshortwave currently over the UT/CO/WY triple point that will track a \nsurface low south of the UP this weekend and bring chances of rain \nto the UP Friday and Saturday. The NBM continues to trend the core \nof heavy rainfall south, though the LREF has wavered back and forth \nsome over the past few runs. The 00Z RRFS doesn't even guarantee \nprecip for the vast majority of the UP, only showing measurable \nprecip for southern Menominee County and pockets of the interior \nwest. While that solution is on the dry side of the distribution, it \nis within the realm of possibilities. Current chances of a tenth of \nan inch of precip or higher in the LREF are around 50% or higher for \nthe US-2 corridor and south. The 25th-75th percentile precipitation \naccumulation remains high as the 25th percentile shows very little \nprecip for all except for Menominee County, though the 75th \npercentile shows up to half an inch for Iron River/Escanaba and over \nan inch for the southern half of Menominee County, so some heavy \nrainfall remains possible but is far from guaranteed. \n\nIn the wake of the disturbance, the western CONUS trough will shift \ninto the Northern Plains, weakening slightly. At the same time, \nridging to the north of the trough amplifies, causing split flow. At \nthis time, the favored solution is for the UP to be affected by the \nnorthern branch of this split flow which will lead to persistent \nhigh pressure and dry weather and near-normal to slightly below \nnormal temperatures for Sunday through at least the first few days \nof next week until ensemble spread increases for the midweek and \nbeyond.",
